# <type>(<scope>)<!>: <short summary>  (max ~50 chars)
# |<----  Using a maximum of 50 characters  ---->|


# Body: explain WHAT and WHY, not how. Wrap at 72 chars.
# |<----   Try to limit each line to a maximum of 72 characters   ---->|


# Footer: issue refs, breaking changes, co-authors
# e.g. Closes #123
#      BREAKING CHANGE: /v2/auth now requires a bearer token
#      Co-authored-by: Name <email@example.com>


# ── Conventional Commit types ────────────────────────────────
#   feat      new feature (minor version bump)
#   fix       bug fix (patch version bump)
#   docs      documentation only
#   refactor  code change, no bug fix or feature
#   perf      performance improvement
#   test      adding or correcting tests
#   build     build system or external dependencies
#   ci        CI configuration and scripts
#   chore     maintenance, tooling, no src/test changes
#   style     formatting/whitespace, no logic change
#   revert    reverts a previous commit
#
# ── Rules ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   * Summary: imperative mood ("add", not "added"/"adds")
#   * Summary: no trailing period, lowercase after the colon
#   * Scope is optional: feat(alarm), fix(mailout), ci, docs
#   * Append ! for breaking changes: feat(api)!: ...
#   * Blank line between summary, body, and footer
#   * Body: explain motivation and contrast with prior behavior
#
# ── Ask yourself ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#   * Is this ONE logical change? If not, split it.
#   * Would this message make sense in 6 months via git log?
#   * Should this be a --fixup into an earlier commit instead?
